---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleContent from '@/components/articles/ArticleContent.astro';
import ArticleFAQ from '@/components/articles/ArticleFAQ.astro';
import ArticleStats from '@/components/articles/ArticleStats.astro';
import FreeConsultationForm from '@/components/home/FreeConsultationForm.astro';
import Testimonials from '@/components/home/Testimonials.astro';
import LocationMap from '@/components/home/LocationMap.astro';

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "What compensation can I recover after losing a limb in Washtenaw County?",
    answer: "Compensation depends on how your injury occurred. In auto accident cases, your no-fault insurance provides PIP benefits for medical expenses, prosthetics, attendant care, and wage loss. You can also sue the at-fault driver for pain and suffering and excess damages. In construction accidents, workers' compensation provides benefits plus potential third-party claims. In all cases involving negligence, you may recover damages for medical expenses, prosthetic costs and replacements, rehabilitation, home and vehicle modifications, lost earning capacity, pain and suffering, and emotional distress. We work with experts to calculate lifetime costs, often reaching millions of dollars for amputation cases."
  },
  {
    question: "How long do I have to file a loss of limb claim in Michigan?",
    answer: "Deadlines vary by case type. For auto accident third-party claims, you generally have three years from the date of injury. For workers' compensation claims, you must notify your employer within 90 days and file within two years. For medical malpractice cases involving amputation, the statute of limitations is generally two years from when you discovered or should have discovered the malpractice. However, exceptions exist. Consulting an attorney immediately after your injury ensures all deadlines are met and evidence is preserved."
  },
  {
    question: "Will workers' compensation cover my amputation injury in Washtenaw County?",
    answer: "If your amputation occurred during the course of employment in Washtenaw County, workers' compensation provides benefits regardless of fault, including medical treatment coverage, wage loss benefits (typically 80% of your after-tax average weekly wage), vocational rehabilitation, and specific loss benefits for permanent impairments. However, workers' compensation is typically your exclusive remedy against your employer. You may pursue additional third-party claims against general contractors, equipment manufacturers, or other negligent parties whose actions caused your injury."
  },
  {
    question: "What if my amputation resulted from a defective product or equipment failure?",
    answer: "Michigan product liability law allows you to hold manufacturers strictly liable when defective products cause catastrophic injuries like amputation. You must prove the product was defective when it left the manufacturer's control, the defect made it unreasonably dangerous, you were using it as intended or in a foreseeable manner, and the defect caused your limb loss. Our attorneys work with engineering experts to establish product defects and pursue full compensation from manufacturers."
  },
  {
    question: "How do you calculate the value of a loss of limb case?",
    answer: "Amputation cases involve complex damage calculations requiring expert testimony. We work with life care planners to project lifetime medical costs including prosthetic replacements every 3-7 years, ongoing medical care, physical therapy, and home modifications. Vocational experts assess lost earning capacity based on your career limitations. Economists calculate these losses adjusted for inflation over your life expectancy. We also pursue substantial damages for pain and suffering, permanent disfigurement, loss of enjoyment of life, and emotional distress. Total damages in amputation cases often reach into the millions when all lifetime needs are properly documented."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Loss of Limb Attorneys Washtenaw County MI | Amputation Injury Lawyers"
  description="Washtenaw County amputation attorneys with 45 years fighting for limb loss victims. full compensation for lifetime care, prosthetics, and lost wages. Free consultation. No fee unless we win."
>
  <ArticleContent title="Loss of Limb Attorneys Washtenaw County MI">
    <p>Losing a limb changes everything in an instant. Whether caused by a severe car accident on US-23, a construction site incident, a motorcycle collision, or medical negligence at a Washtenaw County hospital, amputation injuries create profound challenges that last a lifetime. At Logeman & Iafrate, P.C., our Ann Arbor attorneys understand the devastating physical, emotional, and financial impact of limb loss. We've spent more than 45 years fighting for catastrophic injury victims throughout Washtenaw County and all of Michigan.</p>

    <h2>Understanding Loss of Limb Injuries in Washtenaw County</h2>
    <p>Amputation injuries occur in several contexts throughout Washtenaw County:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Motor vehicle accidents.</strong> High-impact crashes on I-94, US-23, or local roads can result in traumatic amputations or injuries so severe that surgical amputation becomes necessary to save your life.</li>
      <li><strong>Motorcycle accidents.</strong> Riders struck by negligent drivers frequently suffer lower-leg amputations when protective gear cannot prevent the catastrophic force of impact.</li>
      <li><strong>Construction accidents.</strong> Heavy machinery, falls from heights, and equipment malfunctions at Washtenaw County construction sites can cause immediate traumatic amputation or crush injuries requiring surgical removal of limbs.</li>
      <li><strong>Workplace incidents.</strong> Industrial accidents involving manufacturing equipment, farming machinery, or commercial tools can sever limbs in seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Medical malpractice.</strong> Surgical errors, delayed diagnosis of infections, or improper treatment at Ann Arbor hospitals can lead to preventable amputations.</li>
      <li><strong>Defective products.</strong> Malfunctioning machinery, power tools, or equipment with inadequate safety features can cause devastating limb injuries.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Lifetime Impact of Amputation</h2>
    <p>Limb loss is not just a one-time injury—it's a permanent condition that affects every aspect of your life. Amputation victims face extensive medical treatment, prosthetic costs, rehabilitation and physical therapy, home and vehicle modifications, lost earning capacity, pain and suffering, and significant psychological impact. At Logeman & Iafrate, P.C., we work with life care planners, vocational experts, and medical specialists to document every aspect of your losses—present and future.</p>

    <h2>Compensation Available for Washtenaw County Amputation Cases</h2>
    <h3>No-Fault PIP Benefits (Auto Accidents)</h3>
    <p>If your amputation resulted from a motor vehicle accident in Washtenaw County, Michigan's no-fault system requires your own auto insurance to provide Personal Injury Protection (PIP) benefits regardless of who caused the crash. However, the 2019-2020 no-fault reforms allow Michigan drivers to choose coverage levels—unlimited, $500,000, $250,000, or $50,000 in medical benefits. Understanding your coverage is critical.</p>
    <h3>Third-Party Liability Claims</h3>
    <p>When another party's negligence caused your amputation, you can pursue compensation beyond no-fault benefits through auto accident third-party claims, construction accident third-party claims, product liability cases, and medical malpractice cases.</p>
    <h3>Workers' Compensation Benefits</h3>
    <p>If your amputation occurred on the job in Washtenaw County, you're entitled to workers' compensation benefits including medical treatment, wage loss benefits, vocational rehabilitation, and specific loss benefits for permanent impairments.</p>

    <h2>Why Washtenaw County Amputation Cases Demand Experienced Trial Lawyers</h2>
    <p>Insurance companies know amputation cases involve substantial damages. They employ aggressive defense tactics to minimize payouts. Our attorneys have decades of experience countering these tactics. We prepare every amputation case for trial because insurance companies know we are ready to try a case.</p>

    <h2>Serving Amputation Victims Throughout Washtenaw County</h2>
    <p>Our Ann Arbor office serves limb loss victims in communities throughout Washtenaw County, including Ypsilanti, Saline, Dexter, Chelsea, Milan, and Manchester. No fee unless we win. We operate on a contingency-fee basis, which means you pay nothing unless we recover compensation for you.</p>

    <h2>Contact Our Washtenaw County Loss of Limb Attorneys Today</h2>
    <p>If you or a loved one has suffered limb loss due to another party's negligence in Washtenaw County, acting promptly matters. At Logeman & Iafrate, P.C., we offer free consultations to evaluate your case and explain your options. Call us at <strong>(734) 994-0200</strong> or fill out our online contact form to schedule your consultation at our Ann Arbor office.</p>
  </ArticleContent>

  <ArticleFAQ items={faqItems} />

  <ArticleStats />

  <FreeConsultationForm />

  <Testimonials />

  <LocationMap />
</BaseLayout>
